1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:08,400
This limiter keeps its reactor in series. In normal operation, the
capacitor compensates the reactor's reactance.

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:17,500
The thyristors are blocked. At sixty hertz, inductive and capacitive
reactances nearly cancel, keeping the normal voltage drop small.

3
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:25,400
Now watch the fault response in slow motion. Initially, current
flows through the capacitor.

4
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:33,000
Detection takes zero point three seven milliseconds. Conduction
starts after zero point four eight milliseconds.

5
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:43,500
Current transfers to the bypass, removing capacitor compensation.
The reactor now limits fault current in this branch. It was
connected all along!

6
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:50,200
Only source one passes through the limiter. Source two feeds the
fault directly.

7
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:57,300
Compare phase A on identical axes: red without the limiter, blue
with it.

8
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:07,000
Symmetrical R M S fault current falls from seventy-nine point nine
six to forty-nine point nine one kiloamps.

9
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:11,600
That's a thirty-seven point five eight percent reduction!

10
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:21,000
The blue trace is three-phase R M S, including D C. The feeder
breaker opens.

11
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:27,300
Removing gate pulses does not force turn-off. Each thyristor needs
its own current zero.

12
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:32,100
Once conduction ends and recovery is ready, the feeder can reclose.

13
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:37,500
After reclosing, the magnified trace shows a damped transient.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,900
Normal operation and capacitor compensation are restored.
